{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'cat .agents/skills/pose-review/SKILL.md'","aggregated_output":"---\nname: pose-review\ndescription: Use for POSE pull-request or code review to verify controlled scope, preserved contracts, security and observability impact, risk-proportional validation, and escalation where applicable. Trigger keywords - review, code review, PR review, review opinion, ultrareview.\nwhen_to_use: Evaluating your own or another author's diff or PR under POSE. Use before commenting or approving to select rules, inspect validation evidence and prior decisions, and issue an actionable decision.\npose_schema_range: \"1-1\"\nclients: agents-skills, mcp, claude-code\ncapabilities: read\n---\n\n# Skill: pose-review\n\n## Required reading\n\n1. [AGENTS.md](../../../AGENTS.md).\n2. [`.pose/workflows/review.md`](../../../.pose/workflows/review.md).\n3. Applicable domain rules; security takes precedence in conflicts.\n\nResolve the selected task with `pose context --task <typed-or-qualified-artifact-ref> --json` before reviewing. Follow its canonical authority and stop on ambiguity, stale binding, unsupported metadata or `transfer-in-progress`. Use the qualified `xref:` scope for cross-project review; every cross-project write requires an explicit `POSE_PROJECT_ROOTS` binding and the current `context_revision` as `--expect-context`. Keep review evidence and its `POSE-Spec:` trailer in the authority repository.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Classify the change as feature, bugfix, refactor, documentation, or mixed.\n2. Run `pose assess tech-debt` during code review to ensure markers (`TODO`, `FIXME`, `stub`, `panic`) are covered by follow-ups or specs. Run `pose assess integrate` when touching inter-module contracts (Protobuf, Kafka, REST, MCP).\n3. Resolve `pose review bundle <scope> --explain` to inspect effective criteria, components and tool requirements.\n4. Run active required tools first, record why each recommended tool was used or skipped, and keep completion tools deferred until attestation.\n5. Select rules with `pose suggest review --path <affected-dir>` for every mapped component.\n6. Search `.pose/knowledge/` for prior module decisions, accepted risks, and pending follow-ups.\n7. Require deterministic `pose validate --strict` evidence matching delivery targets.\n8. Evaluate every required plan criterion, including cross-component boundaries. When the change adds or alters a governance gate, also answer: what is the cheapest way to satisfy this gate in form without producing its value, and what stops that? Point to the adversarial corpus case that proves it (`pose-mcp/internal/cli/testdata/adversarial/README.md`), or record the gap.\n9. Classify findings as critical, high, medium, or low with evidence and expected action.\n10. Run `pose recurrence-check --tolerant --window-days 14`; use recurrence escalation for a matching systemic signal.\n11. Create a handoff with `pose new-knowledge handoff <slug>` for accepted residual risk, monitoring, or deferred action.\n12. Seal the review subject with `pose review bundle <scope> --seal`.\n13. Prepare the mechanical half with `pose review auto-attest <bundle-id> --reviewer agent:<id>` — without `--apply` — and read `review_attestation.pending`: those are the criteria no registered check answers, and they are yours to answer. Then record the attestation with `pose review attest`, giving every judgment criterion a conclusion. `--apply` on auto-attest records only when nothing is pending; it refuses otherwise, and never answers a judgment criterion for you. An explicit attestation records `--criterion ID|passed|evidence` (or `not-applicable` with a rationale, or `finding` naming a recorded finding) and `--finding ID|severity|disposition|action|evidence[|owner|rationale|review-by]`; every finding needs a severity and an action, and an `accepted-risk` or `wont-fix` finding also needs an owner, a rationale and a review date. A `passed` criterion may cite only evidence the sealed bundle contains, of a class the criterion accepts. For a cross-project attestation, use the qualified `xref:` as the target argument to `pose review attest`, include `--expect-context <digest>` with `--apply`, and keep the explicit project binding; a bundle ID alone does not select an authority project.\n14. Run `pose review verify <scope>` and `pose review-check <scope>`; close only when both confirm a valid, approved attestation.\n15. Decide: approved, approved with reservations, changes requested, or rejected.\n16. When Contributor Mode is active, if review uncovers POSE linter false-positives, diagnostic frictions, or rule gaps, stage an improvement proposal with `pose contribute stage --type enhancement --title \"<summary>\"`.\n\n## Output requirements\n\n- Completed Rules applied during review section.\n- Bundle/plan digests and dispositions for required, recommended and deferred completion tools.\n- Severity-classified findings with expected actions.\n- Clear and actionable final decision.\n- Handoff when residual risk is accepted.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'pwd; git status --short; command -v pose; cat .pose/specs/pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks.md .pose/adr/2026-11-22-validate-carries-unchanged-checks.md .pose/workflows/review.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'pwd; git status --short; command -v pose; cat .pose/specs/pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks.md .pose/adr/2026-11-22-validate-carries-unchanged-checks.md .pose/workflows/review.md'","aggregated_output":"/var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-695147164/worktree\n/home/go/.local/bin/pose\ncat: .pose/specs/pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks.md: No such file or directory\n# ADR: Carry unchanged checks into the canonical validation result\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted on 2026-11-22 for `pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks`.\n\n## Context\n\nThe canonical structured result at `results_path` is the only validation evidence that review bundles and closeout load. Producing it means running the whole matrix. `--changed-from` already selects the modules a change can affect: it uses changed paths, declared `dependsOn` edges and the high-criticality policy, and falls back to every module for a change outside all modules. Its record, though, marks the other checks as skipped, so it cannot stand in for the canonical result. In an instance with a slow and partly intermittent matrix, every review round pays for the full run.\n\n## Decision\n\nAdd `validate --carry`. The flag uses the existing canonical result as a base when that base is provably comparable:\n- the same schema, mode, matrix digest and engine version;\n- a head that is an ancestor of the current head;\n- not cancelled;\n- a clean material working copy.\n\nWith a usable base, the run executes the changed-scope selection between the two heads. That selection ignores the canonical result file and the derived administrative records that material bindings already ignore: the engine's closed list, still regular files at `HEAD`. It copies the base results of every unselected module. Each copied result keeps its outcome and states the head where it really ran (`carried_from`) and when (`carried_generated_at`). The delivery reading uses that head for the result. Any unmet condition runs everything and records the reason.\n\nA module may declare `\"selfContained\": true` in its metadata. A change outside every module then stops selecting it. The declaration is the instance's statement that the module's checks read only its own directory and its declared dependencies, so it applies to `--changed-from` too. Without it, the existing safe fallback stays.\n\n## Consequences\n\nA change confined to one module reruns that module, the modules enclosing it (the root module included), the modules beneath the changed path and its declared dependents. Everything else is reused with its true origin visible. Only a changed file that no module owns widens the run to every module that does not declare itself self-contained. Consumers that compare a result's head with a subject head now see carried results as observations of the earlier head, which is accurate. An incorrect `selfContained` declaration can carry a stale result. That risk sits with the instance's explicit declaration and is documented next to it.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- Hashing each check's declared inputs would be more precise. It needs every check's inputs declared and verified, and it is a larger contract.\n- Merging a partial `--changed-from` record into the canonical file by hand would hide which results were reused.\n- Carrying across matrix or engine changes would make a definition change invisible to the evidence.\n# Workflow: Review\n\n## Objective\n\nVerify that a change is correct, production-safe, and aligned with its approved scope and specs.\n\n## Preconditions\n\n- Make the final diff readable through cohesive commits or batches.\n- Provide requirement and spec context.\n- Attach implementer validation evidence, including `pose validate` output.\n- Define acceptance criteria and expected risk.\n\n## Execution checklist\n\n1. Resolve `pose review bundle <scope> --explain` (or `pose_review_bundle`) when bundle policy is enabled; otherwise resolve `pose review-plan <scope> --explain`.\n2. Confirm the semantic scope, patch/tree identity, mapped components and bundle plan digest.\n3. Run required active tools first, evaluate recommended tools explicitly and leave completion-only tools deferred until an attestation exists.\n4. Select the plan's applicable rules explicitly and record them in the review.\n5. Resolve rule conflicts with the most restrictive option, prioritizing security for exposure, authorization, and integrity risks.\n6. Consult `.pose/knowledge/` for relevant handoffs, accepted risks, follow-ups, and decision logs.\n7. Check compliance with specs, contracts, and local instructions.\n8. Review functional correctness and edge cases.\n9. Evaluate security, observability, and performance risks.\n10. Require validation evidence from `.pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json` proportional to risk.\n11. Identify regression and rollout or rollback risks.\n12. Run plan-selected assessments, including tech-debt and integration when applicable.\n13. Classify findings by severity and propose objective actions.\n14. **Produce a handoff** in `.pose/knowledge/` when findings result in accepted risk, post-merge monitoring, or deferred work (`pose new-knowledge handoff <slug>`); link it from the review.\n15. Seal the validated subject with `pose review bundle <scope> --seal`; stop if any input is unclassified or changed since preparation.\n16. Issue a final decision: approved, approved with reservations, changes requested, or rejected.\n17. Record the decision with `pose review attest <bundle-id> ... --plan-digest <sha256> --apply`; signed external envelopes use `pose review attest --envelope <path> --apply`.\n18. Run `pose review verify <scope>` and `pose review-check <scope>`; reject stale, incomplete, conflicting or transitively invalid reused criteria.\n19. Apply closeout only after verification reports `ready-to-close`; lifecycle, state and report refreshes must not change the sealed bundle.\n20. When Contributor Mode is active and review identifies linter false-positives, diagnostic frictions, or POSE rule gaps, stage an improvement proposal via `pose contribute stage --type enhancement`.\n\n## Required rule selection\n\nAttach this section to the review:\n\n```md\n## Rules applied during review\n- Change type: <feature|bugfix|refactor|documentation-update|mixed>\n- Workflow consulted: `.pose/workflows/<file>.md`\n- Rules selected:\n  - [ ] `.pose/rules/security.md`\n  - [ ] `.pose/rules/backend-go.md` (extension `pose-rule-backend-go`, when installed)\n  - [ ] `.pose/rules/frontend-react.md` (extension `pose-rule-frontend-react`, when installed)\n  - [ ] `.pose/rules/kubernetes.md` (extension `pose-rule-kubernetes`, when installed)\n  - [ ] `.pose/rules/documentation-style.md`\n  - [ ] `.pose/rules/knowledge-governance.md` when knowledge or process changes\n- Rationale for each selected rule: <one line per item>\n- Rules not applicable: <list and explain>\n```\n\n## Recurrence escalation\n\n- Trigger `.pose/workflows/recurrence-escalation.md` when recurring rework is uncovered and the threshold is met.\n- Require `recurrence_rework_uncovered` evidence for the 30-day period.\n- Require explicit links from the specialized workflow to applicable domain rules.\n- Require a formal keep, adjust, or discard decision after the 45-day pilot.\n\n## Minimum domain coverage\n\n- React UI: apply `frontend-react`, `security`, and `documentation-style`.\n- Go services: apply `backend-go`, `security`, and `documentation-style`.\n- Cluster deployment or infrastructure: apply `kubernetes`, `security`, and `documentation-style`.\n- Process, spec, workflow, rule, or report: apply `documentation-style`, `knowledge-governance`, and security when sensitive data is involved.\n- Cross-stack changes: apply every touched domain rule cumulatively.\n\n## Domain review checklist\n\n### Security\n\n- Confirm authentication, authorization, and least privilege when applicable.\n- Verify that code, configuration, manifests, docs, and logs contain no secrets.\n- Require applicable vulnerability and secret-scanning evidence.\n\n### Contracts\n\n- Confirm compatibility of public HTTP, event, schema, CLI, and file contracts.\n- Validate forward and backward compatibility for rollout and rollback.\n- Require a spec update for contract changes.\n\n### Observability\n\n- Verify structured logs and metrics without sensitive data.\n- Confirm that probes, health checks, and alerts reflect real behavior.\n- Preserve enough traceability for post-deployment diagnosis.\n\n### Validation\n\n- Require lint, typecheck, test, and build coverage proportional to risk.\n- Require executed `pose validate` evidence and relevant results.\n- Record environment limitations and residual validation risks.\n\n## Editorial checklist\n\n- Use imperative, actionable instructions.\n- Keep bullets short and avoid duplicated sections.\n- Use `check`, `spec`, and `workflow` consistently.\n- Reference explicit files and paths.\n\n## Required outputs\n\n- A review decision with rationale.\n- The inspected effective plan, its component provenance and tool dispositions.\n- An immutable sealed review bundle and separate attestation bound to its exact digest.\n- A completed rule-selection section.\n- Findings with severity, evidence, and recommendation.\n- Recurrence classification by domain and cause with preventive actions.\n- An explicit statement about public contracts and compatibility.\n- Residual risks and safe-deployment conditions.\n- References to executed checks and collected evidence.\n\n## Complete multi-rule review example\n\n```md\n## Review Summary\n- Decision: approved with reservations\n- Change type: feature (Go API, React UI, and Helm)\n- Workflow: `.pose/workflows/feature.md`\n\n## Rules applied during review\n- `.pose/rules/backend-go.md` (extension `pose-rule-backend-go`): verified handlers, context, and error handling.\n- `.pose/rules/frontend-react.md` (extension `pose-rule-frontend-react`): verified accessibility and explicit loading and error states.\n- `.pose/rules/kubernetes.md` (extension `pose-rule-kubernetes`): verified resources, probes, and immutable images.\n- `.pose/rules/security.md`: verified authorization and absence of secrets.\n- `.pose/rules/documentation-style.md`: verified editorial consistency.\n\n## Checks and evidence\n- `pose validate`: passed\n- `go test ./...`: passed in the backend module\n- `pnpm lint && pnpm test`: passed in the frontend module\n- `helm template` and `kubectl apply --dry-run=client`: passed\n\n## Contracts and compatibility\n- Preserved `POST /v1/storage`.\n- Added optional `retentionDays`, preserving backward compatibility.\n\n## Findings\n- Medium: missing queue-saturation alert; add a metric and alert before production.\n- Low: frontend error lacks request-id context; improve observable UX.\n\n## Residual risks\n- Review did not simulate real cluster load.\n- Increase monitoring during the first 24 hours.\n```\n\n## CI failure interpretation\n\n- Treat `POSE check (strict)` as merge-blocking on protected branches.\n- Treat failed required checks in `POSE validate (strict)` as merge-blocking.\n- Attach pipeline artifacts: `pose-check.log`, `pose-validate.latest.log`, and the POSE report.\n- Classify optional-check failures as quality reservations with an owned remediation plan.\n- Reject changes that violate specs, public contracts, security, or safe rollout.\n\n## Phased rollout for unready modules\n\n- Enforce the current required checks immediately.\n- Use `moduleOverrides` to phase adoption without weakening global structural and required gates.\n- Promote optional checks to required by module with an owner and agreed window.\n\n### Optional-to-required promotion protocol\n\n- Select a pilot domain and map candidate checks with owners and explicit risks.\n- Measure success for four weeks and require a baseline of at least 95 percent.\n- Promote only eligible domains through `moduleOverrides`.\n- Update the validation matrix and quality policy in the same change set.\n- Monitor regressions and adjust rollout without removing established required gates.\n- Update specs and rules when rollout changes merge acceptance criteria.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- Resolve or formally accept every critical and high finding.\n- Make the final decision clear and actionable.\n- Support quality and security conclusions with evidence.\n- Preserve approved scope without unjustified drift.\n\n## Reviewer mode\n\n**Objective:** evaluate technical quality and production readiness with emphasis on correctness, risk, and scope.\n\n- **Focus:** functional correctness, spec consistency, regression and security risk, operability, validation quality, and actionable feedback.\n- **Anti-patterns:** approve without sufficient evidence; review style while ignoring functional risk; request unrelated changes; block progress on subjective preference.\n- **Handoff:** state the decision, severity and evidence for each finding, safe merge or deployment conditions, accepted risks, and recommended monitoring.\n","exit_code":1,"status":"failed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"rg --files -g AGENTS.md -g '*security*' -g '*backend*' -g '*delivery-evidence*' .pose/rules pose-mcp; cat pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_carry.go\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"rg --files -g AGENTS.md -g '*security*' -g '*backend*' -g '*delivery-evidence*' .pose/rules pose-mcp; cat pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_carry.go\"","aggregated_output":".pose/rules/backend-go.md\n.pose/rules/delivery-evidence.md\n.pose/rules/security.md\npose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/AGENTS.md\npose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/AGENTS.md\npose-mcp/internal/version/workflow_security_test.go\npose-mcp/internal/pose/security_scan.go\npackage cli\n\n// Carried checks (spec pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks): with a\n// comparable canonical result as base, validate --carry runs the modules the\n// changed scope selects between the base head and HEAD and carries the base\n// results of every other module, each stating the head it ran at. Any doubt\n// about the base runs everything and records why (R2).\n\nimport (\n\t\"encoding/json\"\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"path\"\n\t\"path/filepath\"\n\t\"runtime/debug\"\n\t\"sort\"\n\t\"strings\"\n\t\"unicode\"\n\n\tposemodel \"github.com/harne8/pose-mcp/internal/pose\"\n\t\"github.com/harne8/pose-mcp/internal/version\"\n)\n\n// carryRecord is the run's account of its base (R5).\ntype carryRecord struct {\n\tUsed     bool              `json:\"used\"`\n\tReason   string            `json:\"reason,omitempty\"`\n\tBaseHead string            `json:\"base_head,omitempty\"`\n\tChanged  int               `json:\"changed\"`\n\tSelected map[string]string `json:\"selected,omitempty\"`\n}\n\n// carryPlan is what a usable base lets the run reuse.\ntype carryPlan struct {\n\trecord    *carryRecord\n\tselection *scopeSelection\n\tbase      map[string]checkResult\n\tbaseHead  string\n\tbaseTime  string\n}\n\n// carried reports whether module's checks come from the base.\nfunc (p carryPlan) carried(module string) bool {\n\tif p.selection == nil {\n\t\treturn false\n\t}\n\t_, selected := p.selection.Selected[module]\n\treturn !selected\n}\n\n// carriedResult is the base result of a check as this run records it (R4):\n// unchanged, plus where and when it really ran, kept through chained carries.\nfunc (p carryPlan) carriedResult(id string) checkResult {\n\tresult := p.base[id]\n\tif result.CarriedFrom == \"\" {\n\t\tresult.CarriedFrom, result.CarriedGeneratedAt = p.baseHead, p.baseTime\n\t}\n\treturn result\n}\n\n// planCarry judges the canonical result at resultsPath as a base. A nil\n// selection means every check runs; the record says why.\nfunc planCarry(root, resultsPath, mode, matrixSHA, head string, matrix validationMatrix, modules []validationModule) carryPlan {\n\tplan := carryPlan{record: &carryRecord{}}\n\tengine, engineVerifiable := engineIdentityFrom(debug.ReadBuildInfo())\n\tunused := func(format string, args ...any) carryPlan {\n\t\tplan.record.Reason = fmt.Sprintf(format, args...)\n\t\treturn plan\n\t}\n\traw, err := os.ReadFile(filepath.Join(root, filepath.FromSlash(resultsPath)))\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn unused(\"no canonical result at %s\", resultsPath)\n\t}\n\tvar base validationRunResult\n\tif err := json.Unmarshal(raw, &base); err != nil || base.SchemaVersion != validationResultSchema {\n\t\treturn unused(\"the canonical result is unreadable or has another schema\")\n\t}\n\tswitch {\n\tcase base.ModuleFilter != \"\" || base.StackFilter != \"\":\n\t\treturn unused(\"the canonical result was filtered by module or stack, so it is not comparable\")\n\tcase base.Mode != mode:\n\t\treturn unused(\"the canonical result ran in mode %q, this run is %q\", base.Mode, mode)\n\tcase base.MatrixSHA256 != matrixSHA:\n\t\treturn unused(\"the validation matrix changed since the canonical result\")\n\tcase !engineVerifiable:\n\t\treturn unused(\"this engine build was modified from its recorded revision, so its identity cannot be compared\")\n\tcase base.EngineVersion == \"\" || base.EngineVersion != engine || strings.HasSuffix(base.EngineVersion, \".dirty\"):\n\t\treturn unused(\"the engine version differs from the canonical result's or was not recorded\")\n\tcase base.GitHead == \"\" || !safeGitRevRE.MatchString(base.GitHead):\n\t\treturn unused(\"the canonical result records no usable head\")\n\tcase head == \"\" || !safeGitRevRE.MatchString(head):\n\t\treturn unused(\"the current head cannot be read\")\n\t}\n\tfor _, check := range base.Checks {\n\t\tif check.LimitState == \"cancelled\" {\n\t\t\treturn unused(\"the canonical result was cancelled\")\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\t// The base must have validated the commit it records: a run over a working\n\t// copy that differed from its head proves nothing about that head.\n\tif !base.MaterialClean {\n\t\treturn unused(\"the canonical result did not validate a clean working copy of its head\")\n\t}\n\tfor _, binding := range base.MaterialProvenance {\n\t\tif binding == posemodel.MaterialBindingUnavailable {\n\t\t\treturn unused(\"the canonical result records an unavailable material binding\")\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tif !materialWorktreeClean(root) {\n\t\treturn unused(\"the working copy differs from HEAD in material paths\")\n\t}\n\t// Selection is lexical: a module reached through a symlink aliases another\n\t// path, so its inputs cannot be told apart (review round 4, N9).\n\t// Two modules sharing one physical directory (a case alias on a\n\t// case-insensitive filesystem) alias each other too (review round 5, N13).\n\tif reason := aliasedModule(modules, filepath.EvalSymlinks, os.Stat); reason != \"\" {\n\t\treturn unused(\"%s\", reason)\n\t}\n\t// Lexical selection cannot see through aliases. Rather than chase every\n\t// alias form (review rounds 4–7, N9, N13, N16–N18), a carry needs a\n\t// case-sensitive filesystem and no tracked symlink that links a module\n\t// (other than the root) or a declared dependency with any other path.\n\tmeta, err := loadScopeMetadata(root)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn unused(\"changed-scope selection failed: %v\", err)\n\t}\n\t// Symlinks inside a submodule hide behind its gitlink (review round 8,\n\t// N20): scan each checked-out submodule at its recorded commit, and refuse\n\t// when a module or a dependency lies in one that cannot be scanned.\n\tif reason, err := submoduleAliases(root, head, modules, meta); err != nil {\n\t\treturn unused(\"submodules cannot be read: %v\", err)\n\t} else if reason != \"\" {\n\t\treturn unused(\"%s\", reason)\n\t}\n\tif link, err := aliasingSymlink(root, head, modules, meta); err != nil {\n\t\treturn unused(\"tracked symlinks cannot be read: %v\", err)\n\t} else if link != \"\" {\n\t\treturn unused(\"the tree tracks symlink %s into or out of a module or a declared dependency, so changes cannot be attributed\", link)\n\t}\n\t// A declared dependency outside the repository changes without any change\n\t// the selection can see (review round 10, N28).\n\tfor rel, module := range meta.Modules {\n\t\tfor _, dep := range module.DependsOn {\n\t\t\tclean := filepath.ToSlash(filepath.Clean(dep))\n\t\t\tif filepath.IsAbs(dep) || path.IsAbs(clean) || clean == \"..\" || strings.HasPrefix(clean, \"../\") {\n\t\t\t\treturn unused(\"dependency %s of module %s lies outside the repository, so its changes cannot be seen\", dep, rel)\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tif caseInsensitiveFilesystem(root, os.Stat) {\n\t\treturn unused(\"the filesystem is case-insensitive, so changes cannot be attributed to modules\")\n\t}\n\t// A case-folded directory inside a case-sensitive filesystem is an alias\n\t// too (review round 8, N22).\n\tfor _, dir := range carryRelevantDirs(root, modules, meta) {\n\t\tif casefoldedDirectory(dir, os.ReadDir, os.Lstat) {\n\t\t\treturn unused(\"directory %s folds case, so changes cannot be attributed to modules\", dir)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\t// Check ids must be unique: two checks sharing an id would share one base\n\t// result (review round 7, N19).\n\tif id := duplicateCheckID(matrix, modules); id != \"\" {\n\t\treturn unused(\"the matrix defines check %s more than once\", id)\n\t}\n\tbaseIDs := map[string]bool{}\n\tfor _, check := range base.Checks {\n\t\tif baseIDs[check.ID] {\n\t\t\treturn unused(\"the canonical result records check %s more than once\", check.ID)\n\t\t}\n\t\tbaseIDs[check.ID] = true\n\t}\n\tif err := posemodel.RawGit(\"-C\", root, \"merge-base\", \"--is-ancestor\", base.GitHead, head).Run(); err != nil {\n\t\treturn unused(\"the canonical result's head %s is not an ancestor of HEAD\", base.GitHead)\n\t}\n\tselection, err := computeChangedScope(root, base.GitHead, head, modules, carryExclusions(root, base.GitHead, head, resultsPath)...)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn unused(\"changed-scope selection failed: %v\", err)\n\t}\n\tplan.base = map[string]checkResult{}\n\tfor _, check := range base.Checks {\n\t\tplan.base[check.ID] = check\n\t}\n\t// A module whose checks are not all in the base runs (R3).\n\tfor _, module := range modules {\n\t\tif _, selected := selection.Selected[module.Rel]; selected {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tstack, checks, _ := moduleValidationChecks(module, matrix, \"\", \"\")\n\t\tfor _, check := range checks {\n\t\t\tif check.Program == \"\" {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tid := module.Rel + \"/\" + stack + \"/\" + check.Name\n\t\t\tif prior, ok := plan.base[id]; !ok || prior.Outcome == \"skipped\" || prior.Outcome == \"\" {\n\t\t\t\tselection.Selected[module.Rel] = \"carry: no prior result for \" + id\n\t\t\t\tbreak\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tplan.selection, plan.baseHead, plan.baseTime = &selection, base.GitHead, base.GeneratedAt\n\tplan.record.Used, plan.record.BaseHead, plan.record.Changed, plan.record.Selected = true, base.GitHead, len(selection.Changed), selection.Selected\n\treturn plan\n}\n\n// carryExclusions are the changed paths that are not changes for the carry\n// selection (R3): the canonical result itself and the derived administrative\n// records of the engine's closed list (the same classes material bindings\n// ignore) that are still regular files, or gone, at HEAD. A symlink,\n// submodule or directory in a class stays a change.\nfunc carryExclusions(root, base, head, resultsPath string) []string {\n\texclude := []string{resultsPath}\n\tout, err := posemodel.RawGit(\"-C\", root, \"-c\", \"diff.ignoreSubmodules=none\", \"diff\", \"--ignore-submodules=none\", \"--name-only\", \"-z\", \"--no-renames\", base, head, \"--\").Output()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn exclude\n\t}\n\tfor _, file := range strings.Split(string(out), \"\\x00\") {\n\t\tfile = filepath.ToSlash(file)\n\t\tif file == \"\" || !posemodel.IsAdministrativePath(file) {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tentry, err := posemodel.RawGit(\"-C\", root, \"ls-tree\", \"-z\", head, \"--\", file).Output()\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tfields := strings.Fields(string(entry))\n\t\tif len(fields) == 0 || fields[0] == \"100644\" || fields[0] == \"100755\" {\n\t\t\texclude = append(exclude, file)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn exclude\n}\n\n// validationEngineIdentity names the engine that ran a validation: its version\n// plus, when the binary records it, the VCS revision it was built from, marked\n// when the build tree was modified. Development builds share a version string,\n// so the revision is what tells two of them apart (R2).\nfunc validationEngineIdentity() string {\n\tidentity, _ := engineIdentityFrom(debug.ReadBuildInfo())\n\treturn identity\n}\n\n// engineIdentityFrom builds the identity from build information. A build of a\n// modified tree is not verifiable: two such builds at one revision share an\n// identity while their code differs (review round 2, N4).\nfunc engineIdentityFrom(info *debug.BuildInfo, ok bool) (string, bool) {\n\tidentity := version.Version\n\tif !ok || info == nil {\n\t\treturn identity, true\n\t}\n\trevision, modified := \"\", false\n\tfor _, setting := range info.Settings {\n\t\tswitch setting.Key {\n\t\tcase \"vcs.revision\":\n\t\t\trevision = setting.Value\n\t\tcase \"vcs.modified\":\n\t\t\tmodified = setting.Value == \"true\"\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tif revision != \"\" {\n\t\tidentity += \"+\" + revision\n\t\tif modified {\n\t\t\tidentity += \".dirty\"\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn identity, !modified\n}\n\n// aliasedModule names a module whose changes cannot be attributed lexically:\n// one reached through a symlink, or one sharing its physical directory with\n// another module, as a case alias does on a case-insensitive filesystem\n// (review rounds 4–5, N9 and N13).\nfunc aliasedModule(modules []validationModule, resolve func(string) (string, error), stat func(string) (os.FileInfo, error)) string {\n\tseen := make([]os.FileInfo, 0, len(modules))\n\tfor _, module := range modules {\n\t\tresolved, err := resolve(module.Abs)\n\t\tif err != nil || filepath.Clean(resolved) != filepath.Clean(module.Abs) {\n\t\t\treturn fmt.Sprintf(\"module %s is reached through a symlink, so its changes cannot be attributed\", module.Rel)\n\t\t}\n\t\tinfo, err := stat(module.Abs)\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn fmt.Sprintf(\"module %s cannot be read\", module.Rel)\n\t\t}\n\t\tfor _, other := range seen {\n\t\t\tif os.SameFile(info, other) {\n\t\t\t\treturn fmt.Sprintf(\"module %s shares its directory with another module, so its changes cannot be attributed\", module.Rel)\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\tseen = append(seen, info)\n\t}\n\treturn \"\"\n}\n\n// aliasingSymlink returns a tracked symlink that could hide a change from the\n// lexical selection: one whose path or target lies in a module other than the\n// root, or intersects a declared dependency, or whose target leaves the\n// repository. Symlinks between paths no module owns are harmless: a change on\n// either side already selects the root module and every module that is not\n// self-contained.\nfunc aliasingSymlink(root, head string, modules []validationModule, meta scopeMetadata) (string, error) {\n\treturn aliasingSymlinkIn(root, root, head, \"\", modules, meta)\n}\n\n// aliasingSymlinkIn scans the tree of the repository at repo, at commit, whose\n// paths sit under prefix in the project.\nfunc aliasingSymlinkIn(root, repo, commit, prefix string, modules []validationModule, meta scopeMetadata) (string, error) {\n\tout, err := posemodel.RawGit(\"-C\", repo, \"ls-tree\", \"-r\", \"-z\", commit).Output()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", err\n\t}\n\tvar deps []string\n\tfor _, module := range meta.Modules {\n\t\tfor _, dep := range module.DependsOn {\n\t\t\tdeps = append(deps, filepath.ToSlash(filepath.Clean(dep)))\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\ttouches := func(p string) bool {\n\t\tfor _, m := range modules {\n\t\t\tif m.Rel != \".\" && (p == m.Rel || strings.HasPrefix(p, m.Rel+\"/\") || strings.HasPrefix(m.Rel, p+\"/\")) {\n\t\t\t\treturn true\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\tfor _, dep := range deps {\n\t\t\tif dep == \".\" || p == dep || strings.HasPrefix(p, dep+\"/\") || strings.HasPrefix(dep, p+\"/\") {\n\t\t\t\treturn true\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn false\n\t}\n\tfor _, entry := range strings.Split(string(out), \"\\x00\") {\n\t\tif !strings.HasPrefix(entry, \"120000 \") {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\ttab := strings.IndexByte(entry, '\\t')\n\t\tif tab < 0 {\n\t\t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"unexpected tree entry %q\", entry)\n\t\t}\n\t\tfields := strings.Fields(entry[:tab])\n\t\tif len(fields) != 3 {\n\t\t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"unexpected tree entry %q\", entry)\n\t\t}\n\t\tlink := path.Join(prefix, entry[tab+1:])\n\t\tblob, err := posemodel.RawGit(\"-C\", repo, \"cat-file\", \"blob\", fields[2]).Output()\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn \"\", err\n\t\t}\n\t\ttarget := string(blob)\n\t\tresolved := path.Clean(path.Join(path.Dir(link), target))\n\t\tif path.IsAbs(target) || resolved == \"..\" || strings.HasPrefix(resolved, \"../\") || touches(link) || touches(resolved) {\n\t\t\treturn link, nil\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn \"\", nil\n}\n\n// caseInsensitiveFilesystem reports whether root's filesystem folds case: the\n// upper-case spelling of its .git entry names the same file.\nfunc caseInsensitiveFilesystem(root string, stat func(string) (os.FileInfo, error)) bool {\n\tlower, err := stat(filepath.Join(root, \".git\"))\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn true\n\t}\n\tupper, err := stat(filepath.Join(root, \".GIT\"))\n\treturn err == nil && os.SameFile(lower, upper)\n}\n\n// duplicateCheckID returns a check id the matrix defines more than once.\nfunc duplicateCheckID(matrix validationMatrix, modules []validationModule) string {\n\tseen := map[string]bool{}\n\tfor _, module := range modules {\n\t\tstack, checks, _ := moduleValidationChecks(module, matrix, \"\", \"\")\n\t\tfor _, check := range checks {\n\t\t\tif check.Program == \"\" {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tid := module.Rel + \"/\" + stack + \"/\" + check.Name\n\t\t\tif seen[id] {\n\t\t\t\treturn id\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tseen[id] = true\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn \"\"\n}\n\n// gitlinks lists the gitlinks of a tree: path and recorded commit.\nfunc gitlinks(repo, commit string) (map[string]string, error) {\n\tout, err := posemodel.RawGit(\"-C\", repo, \"ls-tree\", \"-r\", \"-z\", commit).Output()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn nil, err\n\t}\n\tlinks := map[string]string{}\n\tfor _, entry := range strings.Split(string(out), \"\\x00\") {\n\t\tif !strings.HasPrefix(entry, \"160000 \") {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\ttab := strings.IndexByte(entry, '\\t')\n\t\tfields := strings.Fields(entry[:max(tab, 0)])\n\t\tif tab < 0 || len(fields) != 3 {\n\t\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"unexpected tree entry %q\", entry)\n\t\t}\n\t\tlinks[entry[tab+1:]] = fields[2]\n\t}\n\treturn links, nil\n}\n\n// submoduleAliases names a submodule that could hide an alias: one that is\n// not checked out at its recorded commit, or that itself has gitlinks, or\n// whose tree tracks an aliasing symlink.\nfunc submoduleAliases(root, head string, modules []validationModule, meta scopeMetadata) (string, error) {\n\tlinks, err := gitlinks(root, head)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", err\n\t}\n\t// Every gitlink is scanned, not only those holding a module or a\n\t// dependency: one inside a module aliases its paths too (review round 9,\n\t// N24).\n\tfor sub, commit := range links {\n\t\trepo := filepath.Join(root, filepath.FromSlash(sub))\n\t\tif err := posemodel.RawGit(\"-C\", repo, \"cat-file\", \"-e\", commit+\"^{commit}\").Run(); err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn fmt.Sprintf(\"submodule %s is not checked out at its recorded commit, so aliases in it cannot be ruled out\", sub), nil\n\t\t}\n\t\tnested, err := gitlinks(repo, commit)\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn \"\", err\n\t\t}\n\t\tif len(nested) > 0 {\n\t\t\treturn fmt.Sprintf(\"submodule %s has nested submodules, so aliases in it cannot be ruled out\", sub), nil\n\t\t}\n\t\tlink, err := aliasingSymlinkIn(root, repo, commit, sub, modules, meta)\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn \"\", err\n\t\t}\n\t\tif link != \"\" {\n\t\t\treturn fmt.Sprintf(\"the tree tracks symlink %s into or out of a module or a declared dependency, so changes cannot be attributed\", link), nil\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn \"\", nil\n}\n\n// carryRelevantDirs are the directories whose case behaviour decides whether\n// lexical selection is sound: every existing directory on the path from the\n// root to each module and to each declared dependency, ancestors included,\n// since any of them can fold a lookup (review round 9, N26).\nfunc carryRelevantDirs(root string, modules []validationModule, meta scopeMetadata) []string {\n\tseen := map[string]bool{}\n\tdirs := []string{}\n\tadd := func(p string) {\n\t\troot := filepath.Clean(root)\n\t\tfor p = filepath.Clean(p); ; p = filepath.Dir(p) {\n\t\t\tif p != root && !strings.HasPrefix(p, root+string(filepath.Separator)) {\n\t\t\t\treturn\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif info, err := os.Stat(p); err == nil && info.IsDir() && !seen[p] {\n\t\t\t\tseen[p] = true\n\t\t\t\tdirs = append(dirs, p)\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif p == root {\n\t\t\t\treturn\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tfor _, m := range modules {\n\t\tadd(m.Abs)\n\t}\n\tfor _, module := range meta.Modules {\n\t\tfor _, dep := range module.DependsOn {\n\t\t\tadd(filepath.Join(root, filepath.FromSlash(filepath.ToSlash(filepath.Clean(dep)))))\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tsort.Strings(dirs)\n\treturn dirs\n}\n\n// casefoldedDirectory reports whether dir looks names up without case: an\n// entry spelled with its letters' case swapped names the same file.\nfunc casefoldedDirectory(dir string, readDir func(string) ([]os.DirEntry, error), stat func(string) (os.FileInfo, error)) bool {\n\t// An unreadable directory cannot be shown not to fold. Entries are probed\n\t// without following links, and an unusable one is skipped, not taken as\n\t// proof (review round 10, N27).\n\tentries, err := readDir(dir)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn true\n\t}\n\tfor _, entry := range entries {\n\t\tname := entry.Name()\n\t\tswapped := swapCase(name)\n\t\tif swapped == name {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\toriginal, err := stat(filepath.Join(dir, name))\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\t// The first usable entry decides: its swapped spelling names the same\n\t\t// file only where lookups fold case.\n\t\tother, err := stat(filepath.Join(dir, swapped))\n\t\treturn err == nil && os.SameFile(original, other)\n\t}\n\treturn false\n}\n\nfunc swapCase(s string) string {\n\treturn strings.Map(func(r rune) rune {\n\t\tswitch {\n\t\tcase unicode.IsUpper(r):\n\t\t\treturn unicode.ToLower(r)\n\t\tcase unicode.IsLower(r):\n\t\t\treturn unicode.ToUpper(r)\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn r\n\t}, s)\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
