{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_47","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n 'dependsOn' .pose/specs/*changed-scope-validation.md POSE.md .pose/adr/*changed* | head -30; git status --short\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_47","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n 'dependsOn' .pose/specs/*changed-scope-validation.md POSE.md .pose/adr/*changed* | head -30; git status --short\"","aggregated_output":".pose/adr/2026-11-22-validate-carries-unchanged-checks.md:9:The canonical structured result at `results_path` is the only validation evidence that review bundles and closeout load. Producing it means running the whole matrix. `--changed-from` already selects the modules a change can affect: it uses changed paths, declared `dependsOn` edges and the high-criticality policy, and falls back to every module for a change outside all modules. Its record, though, marks the other checks as skipped, so it cannot stand in for the canonical result. In an instance with a slow and partly intermittent matrix, every review round pays for the full run.\n.pose/specs/2026-07-18-pose-changed-scope-validation.md:64:- [x] Define deterministic revision, dependency and policy inputs: `--changed-from <rev> [--changed-to <rev>]` with a safe revision grammar rejecting option injection before Git runs; `dependsOn` edges added to `module-metadata.json`; policy widening for `criticality: high` (ADR `2026-07-19-explainable-changed-scope-selection`). ([reference](https://nx.dev/ci/features/affected))\n.pose/specs/2026-07-18-pose-changed-scope-validation.md:112:  `dependsOn` edges — the non-goal accepts this; teams own edge declaration.\n.pose/specs/2026-07-18-pose-changed-scope-validation.md:116:- [open] Seed dependsOn edges for this repository's real modules (pose-mcp, mcp-enforce) once cross-module coupling needs scoped validation. (owner:@pose-maintainers crit:low review:2026-10-16)\n.pose/adr/2026-07-19-explainable-changed-scope-selection.md:32:  via `dependsOn` edges declared in `module-metadata.json`, or when policy\n.pose/adr/2026-07-19-explainable-changed-scope-selection.md:55:  contracts, codegen); declaring `dependsOn` edges is the team's\nPOSE.md:353:  **Changed scope:** `--changed-from <rev> [--changed-to <rev>]` selects the minimum safe module set deterministically — modules containing changed files (tracked and untracked), transitive dependents via `dependsOn` edges in [`module-metadata.json`](.pose/indexes/module-metadata.json), and policy widening (criticality `high` always runs). Changed names are read NUL-delimited and a rename names both its source and its destination. Each changed file selects its most specific owning module, every module enclosing that owner (the root module encloses them all), every module beneath it (a gitlink or replaced directory changes what lies under it), and every module whose declared `dependsOn` contains it, lies beneath it or is the root `.`, even after that dependency is deleted. A file no module owns selects the root module. Module metadata that does not decode stops the selection (a full run under `--carry`, an error under `--changed-from`). A change outside every module runs every module that does not declare `\"selfContained\": true` in its metadata (uncertainty prefers safe execution; the declaration states that the module's checks read only its own directory and its `dependsOn` modules); unselected checks are recorded as skipped with the selection reason and `--explain` prints every decision. Revisions are confined to a safe grammar; without the flags, full validation is unchanged.\nPOSE.md:355:  **Carried checks:** `--carry --json-out <results_path>` refreshes the canonical result instead of rerunning the whole matrix. It uses the current canonical result as its base when the base is comparable: same schema, mode, matrix digest and `engine_version` (the engine version plus the VCS revision the binary was built from; a build of a modified tree never carries), unfiltered, not cancelled, recorded `material_clean` (it validated an unchanged working copy of its own head: no tracked or untracked unignored material changed and HEAD did not move while it ran) with no unavailable material binding, with its `git_head` an ancestor of `HEAD`, a clean material working copy now, a case-sensitive filesystem with no case-folded directory on the path to a module or a dependency, no module reached through a symlink or sharing its directory with another module, no tracked symlink whose path or target lies in a module other than the root or in a declared `dependsOn` (or whose target leaves the repository), every submodule's tree included (a submodule that is not checked out or has nested submodules refuses), no declared dependency outside the repository, and no check id defined twice. Changed names are read with every gitlink shown, whatever the Git configuration. The head is pinned before planning, and a run whose HEAD moved records its material bindings as unavailable. It runs the modules the changed-scope rules select between the base head and `HEAD`; the canonical result file and the derived administrative records that material bindings ignore (data files that are still regular files in the derived-record directories under `.pose/`: indexes, results, state, assessments and review records; the validation matrix stays material) are not changes. A module whose checks are not all in the base also runs. Every other check keeps the base result, its outcome included, and records `carried_from` (the head it ran at, kept through chained carries) and `carried_generated_at`; the delivery index reads that head for the result, so evidence freshness judges the commit a carried check really observed. A carried failure still fails the run. Any unmet condition runs everything, and `carry.reason` says why. `carry` records the base, the number of changed files and each selected module's reason, and `counts.carried` the reused checks. `--carry` does not combine with `--module`, `--stack`, `--workspace`, `--root-only`, `--changed-from` or `--changed-to`.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
